foreign  happy to be here and I want to talk a  bit about how people experience and uh  respond to being victims of  discrimination  we live in a world in which People's  race or ethnicity affects the kinds of  outcomes that we receive  it's also the case that the kinds of  outcomes we receive depends upon our  gender  whether or not we are gay or straight  various kinds of physical mobility  issues whether we have a physical  disability whether we're particularly  short stature whether what weight  category we belong to all of these uh  influence the outcomes that we receive  likewise our age matters to the outcomes  that we receive  so while we may be against theoretically  discrimination  it is an outcome or a form of social  experience that many people uh  experience it's a kind of stressor in  life  and despite our principled objections to  the Discrimination that people  experience  many of us might believe that people  clay are a little bit overly ready to  claim discrimination as a cause of their  negative outcomes  that in effect We Believe  widely that people who are victims of  discrimination are just  um playing the victim card that they are  attempting to avoid responsibility for  their negative outcomes and uh instead  are avoiding responsibility  why is it that this is unlikely to be  the case first of all we know that there  are social costs  whenever a member of a disadvantaged  group claims to be experiencing  discrimination Advantage group members  often attempt to explain that away as  due to anything except discrimination  so uh it's not just that Advantage group  members  um dislike to hear claims of  discrimination it's also the case that  disadvantaged group members are well  aware of the credibility problem that  they have  and so our own research has revealed  that disadvantaged group members fear  when one of their own claims to be a  victim of discrimination they fear that  they will be made to look  um badly uh that they will be made to  look like whiners and complainers so  they too often negatively evaluate  members of their own group who who  report discrimination  so the point here is that nobody  advantaged or disadvantaged is very  happy hearing about discrimination  claims  so we have lots of reasons here to  believe that there are social costs but  what I want to emphasize today are the  psychological costs of experiencing and  complaining about discrimination  what are the consequences the  psychological consequences of perceiving  yourself as a victim of discrimination  well first of all experiencing  discrimination is a form of exclusion  it's saying you are not allowed you are  not permitted  you uh secondly experiencing  discrimination is a kind of devaluation  in the broader society that you aren't  uh respected and valued both of those  are conditions of poor psychological  well-being we know that for people to  thrive they need to feel included and  valued and respected  in addition we would expect that  perceived discrimination will have  negative consequences for psychological  well-being precisely because it  undermines any sense of control it  communicates that you are powerless to  control and obtain positive outcomes  desired outcomes that they aren't under  your control  so for those reasons we would certainly  expect that perceived discrimination and  the more you perceive it as pervasive uh  the more harm it will do to  psychological well-being  to address this these claims what we did  was a giant meta-analysis that is where  we quantitatively assess the huge  literature that has addressed this  question over the years  it involves more than 144 000 people of  many different groups disadvantaged and  advantaged groups and overall what we  find is that there is a negative effect  of perceived discrimination on all sorts  of measures of psychological well-being  everything from measures of depression  to measures of Life satisfaction and  self-esteem feelings of control and so  on and the next question is though for  whom is perceived discrimination uh  particularly harmful does it matter does  it differ for different kinds of groups  well the first question is uh whether or  not it matters for members of  historically disadvantaged groups what  you see here is that the effect of  perceived discrimination in  disadvantaged groups on well-being is  reliably more negative that is worse  than it is for advantaged groups  but we might want to look at different  uh look at what kinds of disadvantaged  groups have been studied  what you can see is that there are many  faces of discrimination you can  experience discrimination based upon  your gender race uh sexuality and so  forth what you see here is that the  effects of perceived discrimination are  weakest for uh discrimination based on  sexism and racism those are groups that  have had civil rights movements and  attempted to create a collective  identity and struggled against  discrimination whereas for the other  groups that are the relationship is  significantly stronger and more negative  particularly the the most negative is  for those with a physical disability  now most of the studies in the  literature that have looked at the uh  these different faces of discrimination  have especially uh examined the effects  for racism or different forms of uh  ethnic groups  so we uh wanted to look at what are the  consequences for different ethnic groups  so just looking at those studies first  of all the one thing we find and the  only time in which perceived  discrimination is unrelated to  psychological well-being is for white  participants  this is a group for whom uh  discrimination is not very pervasive  however for all the other ethnic groups  uh we see that the more you perceive  pervasive discrimination uh the worst  psychological well-being is it's  particularly and significantly worse for  groups that have not had any any kind of  successful Collective movements to bring  about change that is for Arab Muslims  Asians uh and other immigrant groups  so you've been turned down you didn't  get that job you didn't get that  promotion you've been refused uh being  able to participate in desirable  activities because you have a physical  disability you've been denied a an  apartment  um in a desirable neighborhood uh and so  forth you these are all objective  experiences of discrimination that  people may have  but what we want to understand and what  researchers in the work I've showed you  so far have looked at is people's  perceived discrimination so uh our  perceptions and the objective events  that we experience may not be the same  thing so just to take a silly example  when I was a little girl growing up in  St John New Brunswick it was the time of  the big American space program and we  all watched on television as Neil  Armstrong walked on the moon and I was  so thrilled I remember telling my dad at  dinner that I want to be an astronaut  when I grow up and he told me girls  can't be astronauts that but you could  be if you want to fly you could be an  airline stewardess  now I thought okay that's that's good  um you know flying to the Moon flying to  Saskatoon no big difference it's okay I  can do that the point here is I had  objectively been told about things that  I would be excluded from by my gender  but I didn't perceive it as  discrimination I thought airline  stewardess was pretty good idea  okay so what we want to understand is  what's the distinction between  objectively experiencing Encounters of  discrimination and uh people's  subjective interpretation of their  experience so for that we need  experiments we need experiments in which  we deliver to people The Identical  objective event and then we want to know  what's the consequences of perceiving it  as discrimination or as perceiving it as  due to something else  so there are two uh there's fewer  experiments but there's still a  substantial number of them in the  literature that have done exactly that  they come in two different varieties one  variety of experiments  um are of the sort that  uh the experimenter always delivers to  people a very negative outcome and then  induces some people to believe it's due  to their group membership so for example  um I don't get some nice outcome I'm  giving negative feedback and I'm induced  to believe it's because they're  prejudiced against women or I I still  don't get that negative outcome and so  on or I'm induced to believe I didn't  get that negative outcome because I  don't have the ability and skills so I  didn't get that positive outcome  okay uh what you see here and this is a  very good test of this self-protection  idea what you see in all of these  experiments is we see no significant  difference in concluding that my outcome  is due to discrimination or My outcome  is due to uh I didn't deserve that good  outcome  so there's no self this is very good  evidence that there's no self-protection  to be had in claiming discrimination  there's a next set of uh both of them  are pretty uh unpleasant conclusions to  arrive at  the next set of experiments are very  different they too deliver a negative  outcome to people but they vary uh  something more broadly were told uh in  one case that discrimination against  women for example is pervasive uh it's  everywhere uh or it's we learn that  discrimination against women is a pretty  rare thing it doesn't really happen very  much anymore it's isolated  so here what we see is truly negative  causal effects of perceiving yourself as  a victim of discrimination that's  pervasive across context so if you  believe these outcomes reflect broader  devaluation of your group  the same outcome when it's interpreted  is due to discrimination produces  negative harm to psychological  well-being  okay well how then if people experience  discrimination and continue to  experience it how do we successfully  cope with it  we know that there's a negative  consequences for well-being of  perceiving pervasive discrimination  but we also know from a variety a whole  host of studies that have looked at  different disadvantaged groups that to  the extent that they form a positive  minority identity this helps to  alleviate those negative consequences of  perceiving discrimination  so what can organizations do to help  people who  encounter discrimination  pervasively across context if we know  that Minority identity is so important  one organization that we've worked with  I'll just take an example is Little  People of America  this is a group of people who have a  genetic  disorder that produces very short  stature and there are people who  experience very severe discrimination in  the marketplace and in public spaces and  so forth  what we've found is that this  organization  is able to help people partly because  people with many forms of members of  disadvantaged groups are geographically  dispersed and  um are unable to  see that there are others who share  their condition and that it's a valuable  it's okay to be disabled as long as one  can take pride in that identity so  little people of America have been very  successful in helping their members to  improve their psychological well-being  by encouraging a positive uh disabled  and proud minority identity  so I just want to leave you with and  return to the the point I started with  in a sense the myth that many of us may  hold that uh perceiving discrimination  is an easy easy thing to do it's an easy  way out it's a way of explaining and  avoiding responsibility for our uh poor  outcomes  that we saw here no evidence that  supports this at all that in fact  claiming discrimination is a very  difficult uh thing to do there are  social and psychological costs  associated with doing so and to the  extent that we can honestly and help  minority group members who are  experiencing the Discrimination uh to uh  take pride in their uh identity  we will go a long way to celebrating  diversity Among Us thank you

